nge the subject. M's \" I was trying to open up\" is valid too—they didn't know the user's triggers. So who's the ass here? Neither fully. The user set a boundary poorly, but M overreacted. B failed as a mediator. But the user isn't \" an ass\"— they're human. The real issue is lack of communication about triggers. *Pausing to check Jewish perspective*... in Jewish ethics, there's \" * lo telech rachil b' amecha\"* ( Leviticus 19:16)— don't go about as a talebearer, but also \" * v' ahavta l' reacha kamocha\"* ( love your neighbor as yourself). The user was trying to love themselves first, which is necessary. But they could've done it more gently. Final verdict: Not an ass. The situation was a collision of unspoken needs. The user's reaction was human, not malicious. M's overreaction doesn't make the user \" the ass\"— it's a shared miscommunication. ...But the user should reflect: next time, say \" I'm not in a good place to discuss this right now\" instead of \" this subject isn't good for me\"— the phrasing matters. And B should've been more attuned to the user's history. *Double-checking*... yes, AITA is \" am I the asshole?\" and the answer is no. The user wasn't being an asshole—they were self-protecting. M's response was disproportionate, but the user's phrasing could've been better. Still, not \" ass\" behavior. *Final thought*... in AITA terms, \" not an ass\" is 0. \" Ass\" is 1. So 0.",
            "reasoning_details": [
